WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.480
Welcome to Grade 8 English, Unit
7: Historical Figures and Legacy.

2
00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:08.180
Welcome to Unit 7 of your Year 8 English course.

3
00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:09.800
In this unit,

4
00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:16.280
you will explore the theme of Historical Figures and Legacy
- a topic that invites you to think about how individuals

5
00:00:16.280 --> 00:00:20.420
shape history and how their actions echo through generations.

6
00:00:21.780 --> 00:00:29.040
You will read a powerful story set in 1948 East Africa,
learn essential grammar structures for narrating past events,

7
00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:34.780
and build a rich vocabulary for discussing
argument, leadership, emotion, and change.

8
00:00:35.220 --> 00:00:40.940
In this short lecture, preview the key
ideas and prepare for the independent lesson.

9
00:00:41.840 --> 00:00:43.500
By the end of this unit,

10
00:00:43.940 --> 00:00:51.020
you will be able to use the past simple and past continuous
tenses correctly to narrate events and describe actions in

11
00:00:51.020 --> 00:00:52.080
progress in the past.

12
00:00:52.840 --> 00:00:59.100
you will also form and use the past perfect tense to
show which action happened first when telling a story.

13
00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:06.700
you will also convert direct speech into reported speech,
adjusting pronouns, verb tenses, and word order correctly.

14
00:01:07.520 --> 00:01:09.260
Keep these goals in mind as you work.

15
00:01:09.740 --> 00:01:18.700
Your vocabulary work begins with these words: benign,
reprieve, defect, disposition, systematic, cache.

16
00:01:19.360 --> 00:01:24.140
Listen to each pronunciation in the
vocabulary lab, study its meaning in context,

17
00:01:24.540 --> 00:01:28.040
and use the word accurately in
your own discussion and writing.

18
00:01:28.820 --> 00:01:35.340
The reading sequence includes Historical Figures and
Legacy: source text 1 and Historical Figures and Legacy:

19
00:01:35.920 --> 00:01:36.740
source text 2.

20
00:01:37.560 --> 00:01:38.380
Read actively.

21
00:01:38.940 --> 00:01:42.920
Identify each text's central ideas,
notice viewpoint and reliability,

22
00:01:43.400 --> 00:01:46.880
and support every inference with
relevant evidence from the text.

23
00:01:47.460 --> 00:01:50.020
The first language focus is How to Form Them.

24
00:01:51.300 --> 00:01:56.520
Past Simple - Regular Verbs: Add
-ed to the base form of the verb.

25
00:01:57.360 --> 00:02:00.340
play -> played Notice the structure in the model examples,

26
00:02:00.920 --> 00:02:04.040
then apply it deliberately in
connected sentences and paragraphs.

27
00:02:04.820 --> 00:02:08.660
In speaking, you will complete Speaking 1: Story Retelling.

28
00:02:08.960 --> 00:02:13.760
Organise your ideas, use evidence
and respond clearly to questions.

29
00:02:14.300 --> 00:02:17.560
In writing, you will plan, draft, check and revise.

30
00:02:18.300 --> 00:02:20.960
Use the success criteria before submitting your work.

31
00:02:21.320 --> 00:02:22.380
You are ready to begin.

32
00:02:22.940 --> 00:02:28.500
Start with the vocabulary lab, continue through
reading and comprehension, apply the grammar focus,

33
00:02:28.500 --> 00:02:31.280
and complete the speaking and writing practices.

34
00:02:31.880 --> 00:02:36.420
Use AI English for hints and feedback, and
bring your questions to the live sessions.
